- ID display sites in admin-slint:
  1. `UserItem.parent` → parent-entry LineEdit (user ID). Also `UserItem.id` is not displayed (used as callback arg). But parent is.
  2. `HoldItem.id` → legal-holds list Text.
  3. `legal-hold-selected-id` (state only, not displayed? `legal-hold-state` shows status; selected-id not displayed).
  4. `legal-hold-view-id` LineEdit (input, displayed) — should display normalized digits? It's user input; we set it after apply to hold_id. Normalize the id we set: `ui.set_legal_hold_view_id(latin_digits(&hold_id).into())`. But careful: then when the user clicks View, the callback passes the normalized string to `load_hold_detail`, which compares against canonical id — if original had Persian digits, normalization would break lookup. But Worker IDs are UUIDs (ASCII), so fine. Still, "display-only" rule: never feed normalized value back to API. Better to keep the input as canonical and not normalize there. Hmm. The ID displayed in a LineEdit is both input and display; normalizing would violate display-only. Skip view-id input; apply only to read-only displays (HoldItem.id, parent LineEdit, profile owner/parent/team?).
  5. `ProfileItem.owner` → form-owner LineEdit (display+input). `ProfileItem.parent` → form-parent. `ProfileItem.teams` → form-teams (opaque strings, mostly IDs).
     Applying latin_digits in Rust when building model items means the UI value is normalized; if user then edits and saves, the normalized value goes back to Worker — violates display-only. But these fields are editable inputs; Tauri IdText is for read-only display of IDs. Risky. For profile owner, Tauri disables owner field for existing profile (display-only); in Slint it's editable. Normalizing when populating from server could change canonical data if saved. I'll limit to read-only displays: HoldItem.id and UserItem.parent? Parent is in a LineEdit (editable) too. Hmm.

  Where are genuinely read-only ID displays? 
